From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: mmc, eventvwr, resmon, cleanmgr, msinfo32, .msc: the administrative tools' Windows names

Windows administrators reach their tools by name: services.msc,
eventvwr.msc, devmgmt.msc, diskmgmt.msc and compmgmt.msc in the Run box or
cmd, eventvwr.exe, msinfo32.exe, resmon.exe and cleanmgr.exe from scripts
and programs (CreateProcess, which searches system32 and never App Paths),
and the Start menu's Windows Administrative Tools. Wine has none of them but
msinfo32 (an About box).

- mmc.exe, eventvwr.exe, resmon.exe and cleanmgr.exe are system32 (and
  syswow64) launchers for what App Paths registers for their own names --
  sg-shell's console host and tools -- sharing calc's handoff.c (0120).
- msinfo32.exe hands off the same way (as taskmgr.exe does, 0121); with
  nothing registered, Wine's About box as before.
- wineboot writes services.msc, eventvwr.msc, devmgmt.msc, diskmgmt.msc and
  compmgmt.msc into system32 and syswow64: a small MMC_ConsoleFile naming
  the console in a StainedGlass element. A file of the same name that is not
  ours is never overwritten.
- wine.inf: .msc is MSCFile, opened with "mmc.exe" "%1" %* (no no-clobber
  flag, so wineboot -u repairs existing prefixes), and ProfileItems make the
  Administrative Tools shortcuts in the all-users Start menu: Computer
  Management, Device Manager, Disk Cleanup, Disk Management, Event Viewer,
  Registry Editor, Resource Monitor, Services, System Information.

Gate: test/admintools-gate.sh.
